  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nIssued at 246 PM MDT Thu Jul 30 2026\n\nSynoptically for the Long Term period, on Saturday there is a \nridge that will begin to transition to more zonal flow for the \nrest of the period. Throughout the week there will be some \nembedded shortwave disturbances moving through that will create \nsome precipitation chances for the County Warning Area (CWA).\n\nOverall for the Long Term forecast, it is looking to be hot, \ndry, and windy. High temperatures for this period are forecast \nto be in the high 90s, with Monday potentially reaching the low \n100s. Winds look to be breezy to gusty and mainly form the south\nto east for the majority of the CWA as shortwaves move through \nthe region.\n\nMonday does have some fire weather concerns. Monday's highs are\nforecast to be in the high 90s to low 100s for the majority of \nthe CWA. Relative humidity values are looking to be in the high \nteens to low 20s, particularly for areas west of KS Hwy 27. \nLooking at the wind gusts, they are currently forecast to be in \nthe range of 20-40 mph for the majority of the CWA. Moving to \nGFDI, values mainly for Eastern Colorado are in the 30-59, which\nwould promote very high fire growth. Another concern is the \nthreat of dry lightning from any thunderstorms that develop over\nEastern Colorado. With these conditions elevated fire weather \nis possible; however there are some discrepancies regarding the \ntiming of when all the ingredients for elevated criteria will be\nmet at the same time.",
